**Coding part: symmetric difference of two arrays.** Clarify first: elements present in exactly one array; should duplicates inside one array be kept? I assume the result is a set of distinct values.

```java
int[] merge(int[] a, int[] b) {
    Set<Integer> sa = Arrays.stream(a).boxed().collect(Collectors.toSet());
    Set<Integer> sb = Arrays.stream(b).boxed().collect(Collectors.toSet());
    return Stream.concat(
            sa.stream().filter(x -> !sb.contains(x)),
            sb.stream().filter(x -> !sa.contains(x)))
        .mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
}
```

Time is O(n + m) and space O(n + m). Using `List.contains` inside the filter would give O(n*m), which is the common mistake. Mention that `Collectors.toSet()` gives no ordering guarantee; use `LinkedHashSet` if order matters.

**Spring questions, short answers.**
- **Lifecycle:** instantiate, populate dependencies, aware callbacks, `@PostConstruct`, `InitializingBean`, bean is ready, then `@PreDestroy` on shutdown.
- **`@Component` vs `@Bean`:** `@Component` marks a class of your own for classpath scanning; `@Bean` is a method in a `@Configuration` class that returns an object, used for third-party classes or when construction needs custom logic.
- **Status codes:** 200 OK, 201 Created (with `Location`), 204 No Content, 400 bad input, 401 unauthenticated, 403 forbidden, 404 not found, 409 conflict, 500 server error.

**Fail-fast vs fail-safe.** Fail-fast iterators (`ArrayList`, `HashMap`) track a `modCount` and throw `ConcurrentModificationException` if the structure changes during iteration. This is best-effort, not guaranteed. Fail-safe (more precisely weakly consistent) iterators, as in `CopyOnWriteArrayList` or `ConcurrentHashMap`, iterate over a snapshot or tolerate concurrent changes and never throw, but may not show the latest updates.
